---
// The Four Laws content's only conversion point.
//
// This is the deeper material on the site — thirteen pages, three tools, a
// glossary and an FAQ — and until this component existed it captured nothing.
// Someone who has just read the Full Reference or worked through the glossary
// is the warmest reader any page on this site will get, and they were leaving
// with nowhere to go but a nav link.
//
// The link is root-relative (/#notify) rather than an absolute URL — from the
// two-site era, this used to cross from fourlaws.thejonmartin.com over to
// thejonmartin.com's own form, which is also the reason it linked out instead
// of duplicating the form here. Same reasoning still applies (one waitlist,
// one link, not a duplicated form on every content page) — it just no longer
// needs to be absolute, since it's the same site now.
---

<aside class="cta-block">
	<span class="label kicker">From the author</span>
	<p class="lede">
		These laws are the vocabulary I use in the field. If you're running a
		founder-led professional services firm and something structural keeps
		breaking, I write diagnoses — not implementations.
	</p>
	<a class="cta" href="/#notify">
		See what that is &rarr;
	</a>
</aside>

<style>
	/* Sage left rule rather than mustard: this appears on every content page, and
	   an accent that shows up everywhere isn't an accent. Mustard stays reserved
	   for genuine per-page outliers. */
	.cta-block {
		margin: 3.5rem 0 0;
		padding: 1.5rem;
		border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
		border-left: 3px solid var(--sage);
		border-radius: 4px;
	}
	.kicker {
		font-size: 0.72rem;
		color: var(--sage);
		display: block;
		margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
	}
	.lede {
		margin: 0 0 0.85rem;
		font-size: 0.97rem;
	}
	.cta {
		font-size: 0.95rem;
		text-decoration: none;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);
	}
	.cta:hover {
		border-bottom-color: var(--plum);
	}
</style>
